<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · War and march · c. 1250 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="pl">Bogurodzica</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The oldest known hymn in Polish, a prayer to Mary that knights also sang before going into battle.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (traditionally attributed to Saint Adalbert of Prague)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Poles of the Middle Ages</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Polish, Ancient Greek (the phrase Kyrie eleison)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Poland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>composed between the 10th and 13th centuries; first written down at the beginning of the 15th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (medieval hymn, author uncertain (Saint Adalbert of Prague often credited))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Bogurodzica, &quot;Mother of God&quot; (a calque of the Greek Theotokos), is a medieval Christian hymn composed in Poland sometime between the 10th and 13th centuries. It is believed to be the oldest religious hymn, or patriotic anthem, in the Polish language, and it was sung in Old Polish together with the Greek phrase Kyrie eleison, &quot;Lord, have mercy&quot;. Its origin is not clear: several scholars name Saint Adalbert of Prague as the likely author or a contributor, while the two opening stanzas may date from the middle or end of the 13th century or the very beginning of the 14th. The hymn draws heavily on Latin Christian liturgy.</p><p>It was first written down at the beginning of the 15th century. Two records survive from that time: the Kcynia record, with the first two stanzas and musical notation, and the Kraków record, with thirteen stanzas but no notes. Later copies date from the second half of the 15th century and the early 16th, and in 1509 the hymn was printed in Kraków in the Statutes of Bishop Jan Łaski. Since May 2024 the third-oldest manuscript version, from 1456, has been on permanent display in the Palace of the Commonwealth in Warsaw.</p><p>At first the hymn belonged to the Mass and to processions, but by the 15th century it had become a war hymn and battle cry. According to the historian Jan Długosz, Polish knights sang it at the Battle of Grunwald in 1410 and before other notable battles in the years that followed; it also accompanied the coronation of Władysław III of Poland and of the first Jagiellonian kings. Długosz called it carmen patrium, &quot;the hymn of the fatherland&quot;.</p><p>Over the following centuries it gave way to newer patriotic songs, notably &quot;Rota&quot; and &quot;Poland Is Not Yet Lost&quot;. Modern composers have quoted it, among them Andrzej Panufnik in his Sinfonia sacra (1963), Wojciech Kilar in a Bogurodzica for choir and orchestra (1975), Krzysztof Meyer in his Symphony No. 6 &quot;Polish&quot; (1982) and Henryk Górecki, who used its opening as a motto in several works.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer hymn. The first stanza calls on the Virgin Mary, the chosen mother of Christ, to win her Son's favour for the people; the second turns to Christ himself, recalling his baptism, and asks for a good life on earth and life in heaven after death. Later stanzas take up Easter, the Passion and a litany of the saints.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Old Polish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Old Polish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Bogv rodzicza dzewicza bogem slawena maria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother of God, virgin, by God glorified, Mary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Vtwego syna gospodzina matko swolena maria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From your son the Lord, chosen mother, Mary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Sziszczi nam spwczi nam kyrieleyson</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Win for us, send down to us. Kyrie eleison.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Twego dzela krzcziczela boszicze</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For your Baptist's sake, O Son of God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Vslisz glosi naplen misli czlowecze</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear the voices, fulfil the thoughts of man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Slisz modlitwo¦ yo¦sz nosimi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear the prayer that we bring,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">A dacz raczi gegosz prosimi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And deign to give what we ask:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">a naswecze zbozni pobith</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and in the world a godly dwelling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">posziwocze raski pzbith ky’eleyson</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>after life a stay in paradise. Kyrie eleison.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous; the two oldest stanzas, in the spelling of the earliest manuscript. Text from <a href="https://pl.wikisource.org/wiki/Bogurodzica_(pie%C5%9B%C5%84)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (10)</summary><ul><li>IMPORT: the '¦' in line 2.3 ('modlitwo¦ yo¦sz') stands for the manuscript's nasal-vowel letter, lost in the import (read 'modlitwę, jąż'). Line 2.6 'pzbith' is missing the manuscript's abbreviation mark (read 'przebyt'), and 'ky’eleyson' has an apostrophe for an abbreviation sign. Check orig against the manuscript image or a diplomatic edition before publishing.</li><li>line 1.1: 'bogiem sławiena' is read as 'glorified by God'; some read it as 'famed for (bearing) God' [?].</li><li>line 1.2: 'U twego syna Gospodzina': 'from' (or 'with, at') your son the Lord; the petition is that Mary win from him what follows.</li><li>line 1.2: 'zwolena' (zwolona): chosen.</li><li>line 1.3: 'Zyszczy nam, spuści nam': obtain (win) for us, send down to us; the object (forgiveness, grace) is not said.</li><li>line 2.1: 'Twego dziela Krzciciela, Bożycze': 'Bożyc' is the Son of God (vocative); 'your Baptist' is John the Baptist, invoked as intercessor (the Deesis).</li><li>line 2.2: 'napełń myśli', literally 'fill (fulfil) the thoughts', i.e. grant what people intend.</li><li>line 2.5: 'zbożny pobyt' is disputed: a pious life, or a prosperous one ('zboże' once meant wealth, fortune). Rendered 'godly dwelling' [?].</li><li>line 2.6: 'przebyt' (a dwelling, a stay) after life; rendered 'a stay in paradise'.</li><li>Kyrie eleison (Greek, 'Lord, have mercy') kept as it stands.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
